2 CONT Kù (simplified Chinese: 喾; traditional Chinese: 嚳}}), or Dì Kù (simplified Chinese: 帝喾; traditional Chinese: 帝嚳), also known as Gāoxīn Shì (Chinese: 高辛氏), was an Emperor of China during the Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors perio
2 CONC d. He was the son of Jiăo Jí (蟜極/蟜极), the grandson of Shăohào (少昊) and the great grandson of Huáng-dì (黃帝/黄帝), the Yellow Emperor[1]. According to speculative dates (from after 100 BC) he ruled from c. 2436 BC – c. 2366 BC. Dì Kù 
2 CONC established schools and was the first emperor to practice polygamy.
