1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%99%B3%E8%83%A1%E5%85%AC
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st Ruler of 陳 Chen/Tan State (a descendant of Emperor Shun)
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin of Chen
2 CONT
2 CONT From name of the region of Chen (in present-day Henan province). After overthrowing the Shang dynasty and becoming the first king of the Zhou dynasty in 1122 bc, Wu Wang searched for a descendant of the great ancient emperors to g
2 CONC uard their memory and offer sacrifices, to help retain the ‘Mandate of Heaven’, which was considered essential to remain in power. He found Gui Man, a descendant of the model emperor Shun (2257–2205 bc), and granted him the region
2 CONC  of Chen, along with the title Marquis of Chen and one of his daughters in marriage. Gui Man was posthumously named Chen Hugong, and his descendants came to adopt the surname Chen.
2 CONT
2 CONT State of Chen
2 CONT
2 CONT Chen was a minor state of the Spring and Autumn Period in Ancient China. It was a relatively minor state based on a single urban centre near what is now Huaiyang in the plains of eastern Henan province. In its time Chen lay in the
2 CONC  southern part of Chinese civilisation, bordering the semi-barbarous state of Chu.
2 CONT
2 CONT The royal family of Chen claimed descent from Emperor Shun. According to tradition, after the conquest of the Shang Dynasty around 1046 BC, King Wu of Zhou sought out the potter Gui Man (??), a descendant of Shun, and gave him the
2 CONC  feoff of Chen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chen later became a satellite state of Chu, fighting as an ally of Chu at the Battle of Chengpu. It was finally annexed by Chu 479 BC. After the destruction of the old Chu capital at Ying, Chen became the Chu capital for a period.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Early History of Tan and Tan State (陈国) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Recorded history started from Shun Di (舜帝) and he would thus count as the start of the First Generation.  From such start of the recorded history to the beginning of Tan State (陈国) it spanned through 34 generations over a period o
2 CONC f 1181 years.  Each generation averaged about 34.7 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tan State (陈国) and the first Tan, Chen Hu Gong (陈胡公) (the Thirty Fourth Generation) started from1028 BC.  From 1028 BC till 707 BC covering 321 years, Tan State had 12 kings. During this period the State went through a phase o
2 CONC f political stability.  The people within the Tan State lived through a stable and peaceful stage. Tan State (陈国) was then a strong state among the ten states within the Zhou Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Chen Huan Gong (陈桓公), (of the Forty Third Generation) of Tan State (陈国), died in 719 BC during Spring Autumn Warring States, internal bickering and infighting for powers resulted within.  In consequence King Chen Huan Gong (陈
2 CONC 桓公)’s grandson Chen Wan Gong (陈完公){(Jing Zhong (敬仲)} through his second son left for Qi State (齐国), a state in the Zhou Dynasty. From then on Tan State (陈国) faced frequent changes of rulers resulting in the weakening of the Tan St
2 CONC ate (陈国).  Within the reign of Chen Shu Gong (陈戍公) (Forty Eighth Generation) Tan State (陈国) became subservient  to Chu State (楚国), (one of the Warring States into which China was divided during the Eastern Zhou period (770-256 B.C
2 CONC . )), occupying what is now Hubei and northern Hunan. Before this the neighboring Yue State (越) and Wu State (吴国) were strong and Chu State (楚国) did not interfere with the Tan State (陈国).  In 480 BC when the Yue State (越国) defeate
2 CONC d the Wu State (吴国), the Chu State (楚国) took the opportunity to attack the Tan State (陈国) and absolved the Tan State (陈国) into Chu State (楚国). Chen Min Gong (陈闵公) died in the defense of Tan State (陈国). The Tan State (陈国) had a tot
2 CONC al of 25 kings lasting over 20 generations stretching 588 years. 
