1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Taken from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Xi_of_Zhou
2 CONT
2 CONT King Xi of Zhou (died 677 BC) (Chinese: 周僖王; pinyin: Zhōu Xī Wáng) was the sixteenth king of the Chinese Zhou Dynasty and the fourth of Eastern Zhou.[1] His personal name was Húqí.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a successor of his father King Zhuang of Zhou,[2] and was succeeded by his son, King Hui of Zhou.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his time China had dissolved into a multitude of states, only nominally subject to the king, who was no longer even the most powerful figure in China (that was Duke Huán of the State of Qí).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes[edit source]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Cambridge History of ancient China 2.^ Trình Doãn Thắng, Ngô Trâu Cương, Thái Thành (1998), Cố sự Quỳnh Lâm, NXB Thanh Hoá 3.^ ZHOU GENEALOGY (Warring States Period)
