1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Yang Hu''' 羊祜 (221–278), zi '''Shuzi''' 叔子. Wei and Western Jin statesman and literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yang Hu's ancestral home was Nancheng 南城 in Taishan 泰山 (southwest of modern Fei 費 county, Shandong).11 During the Han dynasty his grandfather Yang Xu 羊續 (142–189) served as governor of Nanyang 南陽 (administrative seat Yuan 宛 county
2 CONC , modern Nanyang city, Henan). His father Yang Dao 羊衜 (d. 232) was governor of Shangdang 上黨 (administrative seat Lu 潞 county, modern Licheng 黎城, Shanxi). Yang Hu's maternal grandfather was Cai Yong 蔡邕 (133–192), and his sister Yan
2 CONC g Huiyu 羊徽瑜 (d. 278) was the wife of Sima Shi 司馬師 (208–255). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yang Hu was twelve when his father passed away. He treated his uncle Yang Dan 羊耽 (n.d.), chamberlain for ceremonials, with great respect. By the time Yang Hu reached adulthood, he was broadly learned, a skilled writer, and excelle
2 CONC d at conversation. The general Xiahou Wei 夏侯威 (n.d.) considered him quite unusual, and in 240 arranged a marriage for him to the daughter of his brother Xiahou Ba 夏侯霸 (fl. 230–249). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In his locality Yang Hu was recommended as accounts clerk, retainer, and cultivated talent, but he declined all of these positions. The court official Guo Yi 郭奕 (d. 287) of Taiyuan 太原 called Yang Hu “the Yanzi of modern times.”12 
2 CONC When Yang Hu and Wang Chen 王沈 (d. 266) were recruited by Cao Shuang 曹爽 (d. 249) for the Wei court, Wang Chen advised Yang Hu to accept the offer. Yang Hu refused the position, saying: “It is easier said than done to offer oneself 
2 CONC in service to another person.”  When Cao Shuang was executed by his rival Sima Yi 司馬懿 (179–251), Wang Chen was also removed from office. He said to Yang Hu, “I still remember what you told me.” Yang Hu replied, “I did not realize 
2 CONC that it would turn out this way.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Xiahou Ba defected to Shu in 249, most of his relatives by marriage severed ties with him. Only Yang Hu maintained his relationship with Xiahou Ba and continued to treat him with respect. Shortly thereafter Yang Hu's mother a
2 CONC nd elder brother Yang Fa 羊發 (d. ca. 249) died. Yang Hu was in mourning for more than ten years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the mourning period, General-in-chief Sima Zhao 司馬昭 (211– 265) tried to recruit Yang Hu to his staff. He declined the position. While serving at the gate traffic control office, Yang Hu was appointed attendant gentleman. Bef
2 CONC ore long, he was promoted to supervising secretary and gentleman of the palace gate. During this time, Cao Mao 曹髦 (241–260), Duke of Gaogui xiang 高貴鄕公, who ruled the Wei State from 254 to 260, was a good writer. Many court officia
2 CONC ls presented him poems and fu to please him. In 259, Cao Mao visited the national university and ordered his courtiers to compose a poem. The palace attendant He You 和逌 (n.d.) of  Runan 汝南 (west of modern Xinxi 新息 county, Henan) o
2 CONC ffended Cao Mao  with his composition and was dismissed from office. Yang Hu was caught in the middle between Cao Mao and He You. He kept his distance from both of them, and his friends admired him for this. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 260, Yang Hu was granted the title Marquis of Guanzhong 關中. He was also assigned as director of the palace library. In 264 he was appointed gentleman for attendance for the counselor-in-chief and granted the title of Viscount o
2 CONC f Juping. He and Xun Xu 荀勖 (ca. 217–288) jointly took charge of confidential affairs. Before long, he was promoted to capital commandant. During this time Yang Hu participated in the drafting of a new law code and court ceremonial
2 CONC  protocol. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 265, Sima Yan 司馬炎 (236–290) established the Jin dynasty. He is known by his posthumous title of Emperor Wu (r. 265–452). Because of Yang Hu's meritorious service to Sima Yan, he was promoted to general of the capital army and n
2 CONC amed cavalier attendant-in-ordinary. He was enfeoffed as commandery duke with income from 3,000 households. Yang Hu declined the title of duke and accepted the title of marquis instead. He was then named chamberlain for attendants
2 CONC . Emperor Wu even issued an edict paying tribute to Yang Hu. In 268, he appointed him vice director of the Department of State Affairs and general of the guards. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 269, Emperor Wu was planning a military expedition against Wu. He appointed Yang Hu commander-in-chief in charge of military affairs of Jingzhou 荊州 (administrative seat Xiangyang 襄陽, modern Hunan area). He was named cavalier at
2 CONC tendant-in-ordinary and continued to serve as general of the guards. When Yang Hu was stationed in the south, he established schools and brought peace and order to the local people. He treated the Wu loyalists with honesty and sin
2 CONC cerity and allowed them to decide whether they wanted to remain in Jingzhou or return to Wu. To save military expenses Yang Hu reduced the number of patrol soldiers by half. He opened up wasteland and turned it into agricultural l
2 CONC and to which he assigned some 40,000 soldiers to tend. He also was instrumental in having the able military strategist Wang Jun 王浚 (208–285) named regional inspector of Yizhou 益州 (modern Sichuan). Wang Jun oversaw the naval campai
2 CONC gn against Wu from the upper Yangzi area. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Yang Hu first arrived in Jingzhou, military provisions did not last more than three months. In the fourth year of his tenure there, there was a ten-year store of provisions. On the battlefield Yang Hu did not wear armor, but 
2 CONC preferred to don casual clothes. He also did not have a large bodyguard. One night he tried to sneak out of the military camp. The army supervisor stopped him at the gate and said, “General, you are the commander-in-chief, and you
2 CONC  are in charge of a ten-thousand ''li'' territory. How can you just leave unprotected like this? Your safety is also the state's safety. Only upon my death will this gate be opened.” Yang Hu apologized and thanked the army supervi
2 CONC sor. From that time on, he rarely went out by himself. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 272, Yang Hu was promoted to chariot and horse general, and commander unequaled in honor. He presented a petition declining the appointment. This piece is included in the Wen xuan under the title “Rang kaifu biao” 讓開府表 (Petitio
2 CONC n declining commander unequaled in honor). The court denied his request. In this same year the military supervisor of the Wu State Bu Chan 步闡 (d. 272) tried to surrender to the Western Jin, but was stopped by the Wu general Lu Kan
2 CONC g 陸抗 (226–274). Lu Kang also attacked and defeated Yang Hu's troops. Yang Hu was thus demoted to General Who Pacifies the South. While serving in this post, Yang Hu was instrumental in the expansion of territory held under Jin con
2 CONC trol. He sent military forcces to occupy places of strategic importance, established five new cities, recovered fertile lands, and recruited able men from the Wu state. The people of the Wu state greatly respected Yang Hu and addr
2 CONC essed him as “His Excellency Yang.” Even though Yang Hu and Lu Kang were rivals, Lu Kang highly respected Yang Hu. When he heard that Lu Kang was ill, Yang Hu sent medicine to treat his illness. Fearing that Lu Kang might be poiso
2 CONC ned, all his advisers asked him to reject the medicine. However, Lu Kang took the medicine without any hesitation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 276, Yang Hu was named General-in-chief Who Conquers the South and commander unequalled in honor. Yang Hu devised a new strategic plan to conquer Wu that he outlined in his famous petition “Qing fa Wu shu” 請伐吳書 (Petition reques
2 CONC ting to attack Wu). Emperor Wu accepted his recommendations and enfeoffed him as Marquis of Nancheng 南城. Yang Hu presented a petition, “Rang feng Nancheng hou biao” 讓封南城 侯表 (Petition declining enfeofment as Marquis of Nancheng), i
2 CONC n which he refused the title. Yang Hu's son-in-law suggested that perhaps he should accept this honor. Yang Hu wrote “Jie zi shu” 戒子書 (Letter admonishing my sons) to chastize the younger male members of his family. The military ca
2 CONC mpaign was delayed because of opposition at the Western Jin court from several influential officials allied with Jia Chong 賈充 (217–282). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yang Hu became ill, and he requested to return to the imperial court. He recommended as his replacement Du Yu 杜育 (222–284). When Yang Hu arrived in Luoyang, he was warmly welcomed. In an audience with Emperor Wu he again presented
2 CONC  his strategy on how successfully to defeat the Wu state and win over its people. Shortly thereafter he died at the age of fifty-eight. 
2 CONT
2 CONT While Yang Hu served in Jingzhou, he enjoyed visiting scenic places, especially the Mount Xian 峴山 area (south of modern Xiangfan 襄樊, Hubei). After his death, in his memory local people erected a stele and built a temple at a place
2 CONC  where he used to rest. They made annual sacrificial offerings at his temple. Du Yu named the stele “Zuile bei” 墮淚碑 (Stele of shedding tears) because most visitors cried when they visited his temple. According to the monograph on 
2 CONC bibliography of the ''Sui shu'', the texts of the epitaphs written in memory of Yang Hu were compiled into the ''Yang Hu duilei bei'' 羊祜墮淚碑 (Yang Hu's stele of shedding tears) in one ''juan''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists a fragmentary collection of Yang Hu's writings in one ''juan'' and mentions a Liang catalogue that listed it in two ''juan''. The two Tang histories record a collection of the
2 CONC  same size which was lost in the Song. He also is attributed with the ''Lao zi zhu'' 老子注 (Commentary on the Laozi) in two ''juan'', and the ''Laozi jieshi'' 老子解釋 (Exegesis of the Laozi) in four ''juan'', both of which are lost. Ya
2 CONC n Kejun has collected seven of his prose writings in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''. These include in addition to “Rang kaifu biao,” “Qing fa Wu shu,” “Rang feng Nancheng hou biao,” and “Jie zi shu” mentioned a
2 CONC bove, his “Yan fu” 雁賦 (''Fu'' on wild geese), “Yu cong di Xiu shu” 與從弟琇書 (Letter to my cousin Xiu), and “Yu Wu dudu Lu Kang shu,” 與吳都督陸抗書 (Letter to commander-in-chief of the Wu State Lu Kang).
2 CONT
2 CONT TPK and DRK
