1 OCCU Emperor of Northern Wei (r. 408–452), Emperor Taiwu ("grand and martial emperor")
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Taiwu_of_Northern_Wei
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Taiwu of Northern Wei From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Chinese Emperor Emperor Taiwu of Northern Wei Birth and death Birth date	408 Death date	March 11, 452[1][2] Names Family name	Tuòbá (拓拔) Given name	Táo (燾) Reign Dates of reign (1st)	December 27, 423[1][3]–March 11, 452 Dynasty	No
2 CONC rthern Wei Era name	Shǐguāng (始光) 424–428 Shénjiā (神䴥) 428–431 Yánhé (延和) 432–434 Tàiyán (太延) 435–440 Tàipíngzhēnjūn (太平真君) 440–451 Zhèngpíng (正平) 451–452 Temple name	Shìzǔ (世祖) Posthumous name: (full)	Emperor Tàiwǔ (太武皇帝) ("grand
2 CONC  and martial emperor")
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Taiwu of Northern Wei ((北)魏太武帝) (408–452), personal name Tuoba Tao (拓拔燾), nickname Bili (佛貍),[4] was an emperor of Northern Wei. He was generally regarded as a capable ruler, and during his reign, Northern Wei roughly doub
2 CONC led in size and united all of northern China, thus ending the Sixteen Kingdoms period and, together with the southern dynasty Liu Song, started the Southern and Northern Dynasties period of Chinese history. He was a devout Taoist,
2 CONC  under the influence of his prime minister Cui Hao, and in 444, at Cui Hao's suggestion and believing that Buddhists had supported the rebellion of Gai Wu (蓋吳), he ordered the abolition of Buddhism, at the penalty of death. This w
2 CONC as the first of the Three Disasters of Wu for Chinese Buddhism. Late in his reign, his reign began to be cruel, and his people were also worn out by his incessant wars against Liu Song. In 452, he was assassinated by his eunuch Zo
2 CONC ng Ai, who put his son Tuoba Yu on the throne but then assassinated Tuoba Yu as well. The other officials overthrew Zong and put Emperor Taiwu's grandson Tuoba Jun (son of Tuoba Huang the Crown Prince, who predeceased him) on the 
2 CONC throne as Emperor Wencheng.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father Emperor Mingyuan of Northern Wei Mother Consort Duguhun (d. 420), posthumously honored as Empress Mi Wife Empress Helian, daughter of Helian Bobo, emperor of Xia (married 427, created empress 432) Major Concubines Consort J
2 CONC uqu, daughter of Juqu Mengxun, prince of Northern Liang (married 433, forced to commit suicide 447) Consort Helian, sister of Empress Helian Consort Helian, sister of Empress Helian Consort Yujiulü, sister of Yujiulü Wuti, Chilian
2 CONC  Khan (敕連可汗) of Rouran (married 434), mother of Prince Yu Consort Helai or Helan (d. 428), mother of Crown Prince Huang, posthumously honored as Empress Jing'ai Consort Yuele or Yueqiang, mother of Prince Fuluo Consort Shu, mother
2 CONC  of Prince Han Consort Fu, mother of Prince Tan Consort Fu, mother of Prince Jian Consort Feng, daughter of Feng Hong, emperor of Northern Yan Children Tuoba Huang (拓拔晃), the Crown Prince (created 432, d. 451) Tuoba Fuluo (拓拔伏羅), 
2 CONC the Prince of Jin (created 442, d. 447) Tuoba Han (拓拔翰), initially the Prince of Qin (created 442), later the Prince of Dongping (created 452, killed by Zong Ai 452) Tuoba Tan (拓拔譚), initially the Prince of Yan (created 442), late
2 CONC r Prince Xuan of Linhuai (created and d. 452) Tuoba Jian (拓拔建), initially the Prince of Chu (created 442), later Prince Jian of Guangyang (created and d. 452) Tuoba Yu (拓拔余), initially the Prince of Wu (created 442), later Prince 
2 CONC of Nan'an (created 452), later emperor Tuoba Xiao'er (拓拔小兒), died early Tuoba Mao'er (拓拔貓兒), died early Tuoba Zhen (拓拔真), died early Tuoba Hutou (拓拔虎頭), died early Tuoba Longtou (拓拔龍頭), died early Princess Nan'an Princess Shanggu
2 CONT --------------------
