1 CONT Emperor Gao of Han
1 CONT Birth and death: 256 BC¹/247 BC²– June 1, 195 BC
1 CONT Family name: Liu (?)
1 CONT Given name: Ji ³ (?), later Bang4 (?)
1 CONT Courtesy name (?): Ji5 (?)
1 CONT Dates of reign: Feb. 28, 202 BC6–Jun. 1, 195 BC
1 CONT Temple name: Taizu7 (??), later Gaozu8 (??)
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT (short)
1 CONT Emperor Gao (??)
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT (full)
1 CONT Emperor Gao (???)
1 CONT General note: Dates given are in the proleptic Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar.
1 CONT ———
1 CONT 1. This is the birth year reported by Huangfu Mi (???) (215-282),
1 CONT the famous author of acupuncture books.
1 CONT 2. This is the birth year reported by Chen Zan (??) around AD 270
1 CONT in his comments of the Book of Han (??) .
1 CONT 3. Name meaning "the youngest one". Liu Bang was the third son of his
1 CONT father, his oldest brother was called Bo (?) , i.e. the "First one", and his
1 CONT second older brother was called Zhong (?) , i.e. the "Middle one".
1 CONT 4. Had his name changed into Bang, meaning "country", either when he
1 CONT was made Prince of Han, or when he ascended the imperial throne.
1 CONT 5. Ji was the courtesy name according to Sima Qian in his
1 CONT Records of the Grand Historian. It may be that Liu Bang, after he
1 CONT changed his name into Bang, kept his original name Ji as his courtesy
1 CONT name. However, some authors do not think that "Ji" was ever used as
1 CONT the courtesy name of Liu Bang.
1 CONT 6. Was already Prince of Han (??) since March 206 BC, having been
1 CONT enfeoffed by the rebelled leader Xiang Yu. Liu Bang was proclaimed
1 CONT emperor on February 28, 202 BC after defeating Xiang Yu.
1 CONT 7. Meaning "supreme ancestor". Was apparently the original temple name
1 CONT of Emperor Gao. Taizu, in the most ancient Chinese tradition, going back
1 CONT to the Shang Dynasty, was the temple name of the founder of a dynasty.
1 CONT 8. Sima Qian in his Records of the Grand Historian referred to Emperor
1 CONT Gao as "Gaozu", meaning "high ancestor", perhaps a combination of the
1 CONT temple name and posthumous name of the emperor (doubts still remain
1 CONT about why Sima Qian used "Gaozu" instead of "Taizu", and what the exact
1 CONT nature of this name is). Following Sima Qian, later historians most often
1 CONT used "Han Gaozu" (???), and this is the name under which he is still
1 CONT known inside China. Furthermore, it seems that in the Later Hàn Dynasty
1 CONT "Gaozu" had replaced "Taizu" as the temple name of Emperor Gao.
1 CONT
1 CONT     This is a Chinese name; the family name is Liu (?).
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gao (256 BC or 247 BC–June 1, 195 BC), commonly known inside China as Gaozu (Chinese: ??; pinyin: Gaozu), personal name Liu Bang, was the first emperor of the Chinese Han Dynasty, ruling over China from 202 BC until 195 BC
1 CONC , and one of only a few dynasty founders who emerged from the peasant class (the other major example being Zhu Yuanzhang founder of the Míng Dynasty). Before becoming an emperor, he was also called Duke of Pei (??) after his birth
1 CONC place. He was also created as the Prince of Hàn by Xiang Yu, the Grand Prince of Western Chu following the collapse of Qín Dynasty, and was called so before becoming emperor.
